ddrescue
Usage: ddrescue [-f] [-r N] INFILE OUTFILE MAPFILE

Images a dying disk. It copies what reads cleanly first, logs the bad ranges to
a map file, and skips the rot -- so you rescue the most data soonest, and can
resume later exactly where it stopped. Always keep the map file.

	INFILE OUTFILE MAPFILE   source, image, and the resume log
	-f                       force: allow writing to an existing output
	-r N                     retry bad areas N more times

See also: smartctl, testdisk, photorec
